/-!
# The codimension-one Cartier part on a regular surface

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 0AGB, regular-scheme case; Resolution of
Surfaces, Tag 0BIB, proof first paragraph
(blueprint: lem-per-surface-codimension-one-cartier-part).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A proper closed subscheme of an integral regular Noetherian surface has an
ideal factorization into an effective Cartier ideal and a finite-type quasi-coherent
ideal with finite support consisting of closed codimension-two points in the original
subscheme. Here `Z` is the quasi-coherent ideal of the closed subscheme, and `Z ≠ ⊥`
means that the closed subscheme is proper. `J.support` is the support of `𝒪_X/J`.
The unit Cartier ideal and empty residual support are allowed.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0AGB and Tag 0BIB, proof first paragraph
(blueprint: lem-per-surface-codimension-one-cartier-part).

Proof plan, following the seven steps of the node:
1. The generic point lies outside the proper closed subscheme, since its ideal is
   nonzero in each affine domain. Noetherianity, finite irreducible components,
   sobriety and the reduced-induced-closed-subscheme theorem give the finitely many
   reduced integral codimension-one components D_i of the support.
2. By lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations their ideals P_i are effective
   Cartier and the local rings at their generic points are DVRs. The nonzero proper
   ideal of Z there is generated by a unit times a positive uniformizer power,
   using lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power. Denote its exponent by a_i.
3. Set L = product P_i ^ a_i. On a simultaneous trivializing cover its generator
   is the product of the local nonzerodivisor generators, hence is a nonzerodivisor.
4. At a point x the nonunit local equations f_i are pairwise nonassociate primes.
   The equality of the ideals at each generic DVR implies f_i ^ a_i divides every
   element of Z_x: clear the localization denominator and use primeness repeatedly.
   Successively cancel the distinct prime factors to get Z_x contained in L_x.
5. Define J = (Z:L). Locally write L = (f), with f a nonzerodivisor. Divide the
   finitely many generators h_j of Z uniquely as h_j = f g_j. Cancellation gives
   J = (g_j) and Z = L J. This construction commutes with localization and changes
   of the generator by a unit, so it glues to a finite-type quasi-coherent ideal.
6. J is the unit ideal at the generic point and all codimension-one points: those
   in Z are exactly the generic points chosen in step 1, by the stalk-dimension
   generalization-chain formula. Outside Z, the containment Z_x in L_x and the
   product equality force both L_x and J_x to be unit ideals. Thus its support
   lies in the support of Z and avoids points of codimension at most one.
7. The finite-type-module-support-closed theorem makes the residual support closed.
   The stalk-dimension chain formula bounds local dimension by two, so its points
   have codimension exactly two. A strict specialization would give a chain of
   length three, so each such point is closed. Noetherianity and sobriety give
   finitely many irreducible components of this support, each a singleton, proving
   finiteness. Empty products and empty support require no separate hypothesis. -/
theorem exists_surface_codimension_one_cartier_part
    (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsIntegral X] [IsNoetherian X]
    (hRegular : IsRegularScheme X) (hDim : topologicalKrullDim X = 2)
    (Z : X.IdealSheafData) (hZ : Z ≠ ⊥) :
    ∃ L J : X.IdealSheafData,
      L.IsInvertible ∧ J.toModules.IsQuasiCoherentFiniteType ∧ Z = L * J ∧
      Set.Finite (J.support : Set X) ∧
      (∀ x ∈ J.support,
        IsClosed ({x} : Set X) ∧ ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk x) = 2) ∧
      (J.support : Set X) ⊆ Z.support := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
